    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>Is Wix better than WordPress for beginners?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Wix is better than WordPress for beginners because it is a fully hosted SaaS platform with a visual drag-and-drop editor, 900+ professionally designed templates, built-in SSL, CDN, backups, and no plugin or server management. Absolute beginners can launch a polished website in a weekend on Wix. WordPress has a steeper learning curve because it requires choosing a host, installing and updating plugins and themes, managing security and backups, and occasionally debugging plugin conflicts. If you do not want to think about hosting or maintenance, Wix wins for beginners.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which is cheaper, Wix or WordPress?</summary>
        <p>WordPress self-hosted is cheaper at very small scale and more expensive at large scale. The WordPress software is free, and a budget shared host like Bluehost or SiteGround costs $3–$8/month for a single site, with premium themes typically $50–$80 once and critical plugins $30–$300/year. Wix starts at $17/month for Combo and $25/month for Business. However, once you add a premium WordPress host (WP Engine, $30+/month), a page builder ($49–$199/year), SEO plugins, backup, security, and forms, WordPress often exceeds Wix Business in total cost of ownership. For a fully-featured site up to about 50k monthly visitors, Wix is usually cheaper and simpler.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which is better for SEO, Wix or WordPress?</summary>
        <p>WordPress is better for SEO in 2026 because of its plugin ecosystem (Yoast SEO, Rank Math, Redirection), clean permalink control, taxonomy and custom post type flexibility, page speed tuning through caching and image optimization plugins, and deep developer access to technical SEO like schema markup, XML sitemaps, robots.txt, and core web vitals. Wix has improved dramatically since moving to server-side rendering and now ships solid native SEO features (SSL, metadata per page, sitemaps, robots.txt, image alt text, mobile optimization). The SEO gap has narrowed in 2026 — small sites and local businesses can rank perfectly well on Wix — but WordPress still has a higher ceiling for technical SEO, blogs, and publishers targeting competitive keywords.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I move my Wix site to WordPress later?</summary>
        <p>Yes, you can move from Wix to WordPress, but it is not a fully automated one-click migration because Wix's templates and page builder are proprietary. Text, images, and blog posts can be exported via RSS feed and imported into WordPress in minutes. Pages, layouts, and designs cannot be migrated automatically — you will need to rebuild them in a WordPress page builder like Elementor, Divi, or Gutenberg. A common 50-page Wix-to-WordPress migration takes 1–3 weeks depending on layout complexity. Budget for a staging site, redirect maps, and post-migration QA before switching DNS.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do professionals use Wix, or only WordPress?</summary>
        <p>Both are used by professionals — just for different types of clients. Marketing agencies, freelancers, and studios that deliver small business websites on fixed budgets consistently use Wix because the closed platform eliminates plugin conflicts, maintenance, and security updates between client invoices. WordPress dominates with professional developers who need custom functionality, headless CMS architectures, custom plugins, or complex multi-site networks. As of 2026, WordPress still powers approximately 40%+ of the top million websites on the web, while Wix serves approximately 2–3%, mostly SMBs, creators, and local businesses.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which platform is better for ecommerce?</summary>
        <p>Both can work for ecommerce, but the answer depends on catalog size and complexity. Wix Business + Wix Stores is the better choice for a beginner or SMB that wants a fully-managed online store for up to ~500 products, built-in payments, and no plugin updates. WordPress + WooCommerce is the better choice if you need multi-vendor marketplaces, large catalogs (10k+ SKUs), complex product variants, or deep integration with accounting and ERP systems. WooCommerce has ~30% of the ecommerce plugin market and a massive extension ecosystem; Wix Stores offers fewer features but zero maintenance.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

